### Fixed - SMTP TLS certificate verification failure on macOS

`ssl.create_default_context()` failed with `CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED` on macOS because Python 3.12's default CA file path (`/Library/Frameworks/…/cert.pem`) is typically missing. Also fixed a positional-only argument error in `aiosmtplib` 3.x where `message` was passed as a keyword argument.

**`query_engine/Alert.py`**

- SSL context now uses `certifi.where()` as the CA bundle, with a graceful fallback to the system default if `certifi` is not installed. Verified: TLS handshake to `smtp.gmail.com:465` succeeds with the `certifi` bundle on macOS
- Changed `aiosmtplib.send(message=msg, …)` to `aiosmtplib.send(msg, …)` - in aiosmtplib 3.x the `message` parameter is positional-only (denoted by `/` in the signature); passing it as a keyword raised `TypeError`

**`requirements.txt` / `desktop_app/requirements.txt`**

- Added `certifi>=2024.0` as an explicit dependency (previously only a transitive dep of `requests`) with a comment explaining why it is needed

**`setup.sh`**

- Added `certifi` to the critical-package verification list so missing CA bundles are caught at setup time, not at first email send

